[node name="RoutingBoard" type="Node3D"]
script = ExtResource("1_route")
rules_text = "BLACK CREEK & WESTERN RAILWAY — OPERATING DEPARTMENT

221. The signal stands at STOP. It is cleared for a train only upon an order received, copied in full and read back.

222. An order not understood is not acted upon. Hold the train and ask the sender. No operator clears a signal on what he believes the order meant.

223. The lever is worked by the operator on duty and by no other person. It is never left between its notches.

224. Every copy is filed at the table as it was received, before the next is taken. A correction is entered as such and signed.

225. An operator who leaves the signal clear without an order, or holds a train without cause, will be dismissed.

By order of the Superintendent."

; The authored board: slate, frame, corner blocks, painted track diagram, lever
; base, stops, indicator dial and wall screws. The lever, grip, needle and every
; Label3D below stay live scene nodes — routing_board.gd drives them, and the
; tests read them by name — so only the static furniture became a mesh.
[node name="AuthoredBoard" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
visible = false
mesh = ExtResource("8_board_mesh")

; ---- Plan 6.5: a train-order signal as an 1890 office had it ----
; No painted track diagram: a floor lever on an iron quadrant works the
; semaphore outside, and a small repeater on a plain wooden backboard shows
; what the arm outside is showing. This rig's origin is 0.62 m above the
; floor; the floor is at y = -0.62 here.
